WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.570 --> 00:00:04.770
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.770 --> 00:00:09.370
To all of you Fellow Scholars out there who
are yearning for some more deep learning action

00:00:09.370 --> 00:00:14.660
like I do, here goes the second package. Buckle
up, amazing applications await you.

00:00:14.660 --> 00:00:18.910
As always, links to every one of these works
are available in the description box.

00:00:18.910 --> 00:00:24.230
This convolutional neural network can learn
how to colorize by looking at the same images

00:00:24.230 --> 00:00:27.050
both in colo r and black and white.

00:00:27.050 --> 00:00:31.800
The first image is the black and white input,
the second is how the algorithm colorized

00:00:31.800 --> 00:00:38.370
it, and the third is how the image originally
looked like in color. Insanity.

00:00:38.370 --> 00:00:42.500
Recurrent neural networks are able to learn
and produce sequences of data, and they are

00:00:42.500 --> 00:00:48.430
getting better and better at music generation.
Nowadays, people are experimenting with human

00:00:48.440 --> 00:00:53.240
aided music generation with pretty amaz ing
results.

00:01:05.280 --> 00:01:10.880
Sony has also been working on such a solution
with spectacular results.

00:01:45.130 --> 00:01:50.840
One can also run a network on a large database
of leaked human passwords and try to crack

00:01:50.840 --> 00:01:54.229
new accounts building on that knowledge.

00:01:54.229 --> 00:01:59.020
Deep neural networks take a substantial amount
of time to train, and the final contents of

00:01:59.020 --> 00:02:04.119
each of the neurons have to be stored, which
takes a lot of space. New techniques are being

00:02:04.119 --> 00:02:07.759
explored to compress the information content
of these networks.

00:02:07.759 --> 00:02:12.720
There is an other application where endangered
whale species are recognized by convolutional

00:02:12.720 --> 00:02:17.940
neural networks. Some of them have a worldwide
population of less than 500, and this is where

00:02:17.940 --> 00:02:23.000
machine learning steps in to try to save them.
Awesome!

00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:28.200
YouTube has a huge database full of information
on what kind of video thumbnails are the ones

00:02:28.200 --> 00:02:33.560
that people end up clicking on. They use deep
learning to automatically find and suggest

00:02:33.560 --> 00:02:35.950
the most appealing images for your videos.

00:02:35.950 --> 00:02:41.230
There is also this crazy application where
a network was trained on a huge dataset with

00:02:41.230 --> 00:02:46.090
images of celebrities. A low quality
image is given, where the algorithm creates

00:02:46.090 --> 00:02:50.620
a higher resolution version building on this
knowledge. The leftmost images are the true

00:02:50.620 --> 00:02:55.260
high resolution images, the second one is
the grainy, low resolution input, and the

00:02:55.260 --> 00:03:00.660
third is the neural network's attempt to reconstruct
the original.

00:03:00.660 --> 00:03:05.230
This application takes your handwriting of
a number, and visualizes how a convolutional

00:03:05.230 --> 00:03:08.910
neural network understands and classifies
it.

00:03:16.880 --> 00:03:22.210
Apparently, George RR Martin is late with
writing the next book of Game of Thrones,

00:03:22.210 --> 00:03:27.650
but luckily, we have recurrent neural networks
that can generate text in his style. An infinite

00:03:27.650 --> 00:03:34.250
amount, so beware George, winter is coming.
I mean the machines are coming.

00:03:34.250 --> 00:03:38.420
It is truly amazing what these techniques
are capable of. And as machine learning is

00:03:38.420 --> 00:03:44.670
a remarkably fast moving field, new applications
pop up pretty much every day. I am quite enthused

00:03:44.670 --> 00:03:49.880
to do at one more batch of these! Of course,
provided that you liked this one. Let me know.

00:03:49.880 --> 00:03:53.480
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

